    "expertNote": "# Form 1.120: Property, plant and equipment - Substantive analytical procedures\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: ppe | **Type**: procedure\n**ISA**: ISA 330, ISA 500 | **Connections**: 0 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### CaseWare Audit Form 1.120: Property, Plant and Equipment - Substantive Analytical Procedures\n\n**Purpose:**  \nThis form helps auditors perform substantive analytical procedures to assess the reasonableness of the amounts reported for property, plant, and equipment (PPE). It ensures that any significant variances are investigated further.\n\n**When to Complete:**  \nComplete this form during the onboarding phase, typically after initial data collection and before detailed testing. Use it when you need to verify the accuracy and completeness of PPE balances through comparisons and ratios.\n\n**Key Fields:**\n1. **Expected Value vs. Actual Value**: Enter both expected and actual values for key PPE categories.\n2. **Calculation Details**: Provide the basis for your calculations, such as historical trends or industry benchmarks.\n3. **Variance Analysis**: Document any significant variances between expected and actual values.\n4. **Investigation Notes**: Record actions taken to investigate unusual findings.\n5. **Conclusion**: Summarize whether the analytical procedures support the reasonableness of the PPE balances.\n\n**Common Mistakes:**\n1. **Ignoring Variances:** Not investigating or documenting significant variances can lead to missed issues.\n2. **Lack of Calculation Details:** Failing to provide a clear basis for your calculations makes it difficult to justify conclusions.\n3. **Overlooking Industry Benchmarks:** Relying solely on historical data without considering industry standards can miss red flags.\n\n**Connections:**\nThis form feeds into the detailed testing phase, where any issues identified through analytical procedures are investigated further. It also depends on initial data collection and understanding of the client’s business context.\n\n**ISA Context:**\n- **IS",
