    "expertNote": "# Form 1.2: Property, plant and equipment - Depreciation\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: ppe | **Type**: worksheet\n**ISA**: ISA 330, ISA 500 | **Connections**: 0 in, 2 out\n\n---\n\n### CaseWare Audit Form 1.2: Property, Plant and Equipment - Depreciation\n\n**Purpose**:  \nThis worksheet helps auditors assess the accuracy of depreciation calculations for property, plant, and equipment (PPE) during the initial onboarding phase of an audit.\n\n**When to Complete**:  \nComplete this form early in the audit process, typically after reviewing the client's accounting policies and understanding their PPE records. It’s essential when there are significant changes in PPE or depreciation methods.\n\n**Key Fields**:  \n1. **Depreciation Method**: Ensure it aligns with the company’s policy and industry standards.\n2. **Useful Life Estimates**: Verify that these estimates are reasonable and consistent with prior periods.\n3. **Residual Values**: Check if residual values reflect realistic expectations at the end of useful life.\n\n**Common Mistakes**:  \n1. **Ignoring Changes in Depreciation Methods**: Failing to note changes can lead to incorrect asset valuations.\n2. **Overlooking Useful Life Adjustments**: Not updating useful lives for new assets or when there are significant changes in usage.\n3. **Neglecting Residual Values**: Assuming default values without considering market conditions.\n\n**Connections**:  \nThis form feeds into Form 1.4 (Impairment of Assets) and Form 2.1 (Subsequent Events), as it provides critical data on the carrying amounts of PPE, which are essential for impairment testing and subsequent event assessments.\n\n**ISA Context**:  \nThis worksheet satisfies requirements under ISA 330 (Auditor’s Responsibilities Relating to Work Performed by Component Auditors) and ISA 500 (Audit Evidence), ensuring that auditors have a solid understanding of the depreciation policies and calculations used by the client.\n",
