    "expertNote": "# Form 110.120: Inventories - Substantive analytical procedures\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: inventory | **Type**: procedure\n**ISA**: ISA 330, ISA 500 | **Connections**: 0 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### CaseWare Audit Form 110.120: Inventories - Substantive Analytical Procedures\n\n#### Purpose:\nThis form helps auditors perform substantive analytical procedures on inventory to identify unusual fluctuations or unexpected trends that could indicate misstatements.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nComplete this form during the planning and review stages of the audit, especially when there are significant changes in inventory levels or when risks related to inventories are high.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Inventory Turnover Ratio**: Calculate and analyze the turnover ratio to ensure it aligns with industry benchmarks.\n2. **Gross Margin Percentage**: Compare the gross margin percentage against historical data and industry standards.\n3. **Days Sales Outstanding (DSO)**: Evaluate DSO trends to identify any unusual changes that might affect inventory levels.\n4. **Inventory Composition Analysis**: Review the composition of inventory for reasonableness, such as comparing physical counts with recorded amounts.\n5. **Comparison with Budget/Forecast**: Compare actual inventory levels with budgeted or forecasted figures to spot discrepancies.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Ignoring Industry Benchmarks**: Not using industry standards can lead to incorrect conclusions about normal fluctuations.\n2. **Overlooking Historical Data**: Failing to analyze historical trends can miss significant changes that might indicate issues.\n3. **Not Cross-Referencing with Other Data**: Not comparing inventory data with related financial statements (e.g., sales, cost of goods sold) can result in missed red flags.\n\n#### Connections:\nThis form is typically used early in the audit process and its findings may influence other forms such as physical inventory observation schedules or detailed inventory valuation tests. It he",
