    "expertNote": "# Form 110.2: Inventories - Work in progress\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: inventory | **Type**: worksheet\n**ISA**: N/A | **Connections**: 0 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of CaseWare Audit Form 110.2: Inventories - Work in Progress\n\n#### Purpose:\nForm 110.2 is used to gather initial information about the work-in-progress (WIP) inventory, helping auditors understand the client's processes and controls related to WIP.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nThis form should be completed during the onboarding phase of an audit, typically before detailed testing begins. It helps set up the audit scope and risk assessment for WIP inventories.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Description of Work-in-Progress**: Clearly describe what constitutes work-in-progress in the client’s operations.\n2. **Valuation Method**: Specify how WIP is valued (e.g., FIFO, LIFO).\n3. **Control Environment**: Describe internal controls over WIP inventory management.\n4. **Materiality Levels**: Identify materiality thresholds for WIP to determine audit focus areas.\n5. **Prior Year Adjustments**: Note any adjustments made to WIP in the prior year.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Omitting Details**: Trainees often skip detailed descriptions, leading to incomplete understanding of WIP processes.\n2. **Incorrect Valuation Method**: Misidentifying or misstating the valuation method can lead to incorrect audit procedures.\n3. **Ignoring Control Environment**: Failing to document controls can result in an inadequate risk assessment.\n\n#### Connections:\nForm 110.2 feeds into other inventory-related forms, such as physical count schedules and detailed testing plans. It provides foundational information that guides subsequent audit steps.\n\n#### ISA Context:\nWhile no specific ISA references are listed, this form supports several ISAs, including:\n- **ISA 500 (Audit Evidence)**: Ensuring sufficient evidence is gathered about WIP processes.\n- **ISA 315 (Understanding the Entity and Its Environment and Assessing Risks of Material Mi",
