    "expertNote": "# Form 110.4: Inventories - Merchandise\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: inventory | **Type**: worksheet\n**ISA**: N/A | **Connections**: 0 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of CaseWare Audit Form 110.4: Inventories - Merchandise\n\n#### Purpose:\nForm 110.4 is used to document the initial assessment and planning related to inventory audits, specifically focusing on merchandise inventories. This form helps auditors understand the client's inventory processes and identify key risks early in the audit.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nYou should complete this form during the onboarding phase of an audit, typically before you start detailed testing or substantive procedures. It’s used when you first receive the engagement or at the beginning of a new audit cycle.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Inventory Processes**: Describe how the client manages and controls their inventory.\n2. **Risk Assessment**: Identify significant risks related to inventory misstatements.\n3. **Internal Controls**: List key internal controls over inventory processes.\n4. **Prior Year Adjustments**: Note any adjustments made in prior years for inventory.\n5. **Materiality Levels**: Set materiality levels for inventory items.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Skipping Internal Controls**: Trainees often overlook documenting internal controls, which can lead to a lack of understanding of the client’s control environment.\n2. **Ignoring Prior Year Adjustments**: Not considering prior year adjustments can result in an incomplete risk assessment.\n3. **Overlooking Materiality Levels**: Failing to set materiality levels for inventory items can affect the depth and scope of testing.\n\n#### Connections:\nThis form is foundational and feeds into other forms related to inventory audits, such as physical count schedules (Form 110.6) and detailed test workpapers (Form 110.9). It provides critical information that guides subsequent audit procedures.\n\n#### ISA Context:\nWhile no specific ISA references are listed, this form aligns with IS",
