    "expertNote": "# Form 110.6: Inventories - Other inventories for sale\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: inventory | **Type**: worksheet\n**ISA**: N/A | **Connections**: 0 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of CaseWare Audit Form 110.6: Inventories - Other inventories for sale\n\n**Purpose**:  \nForm 110.6 is used to document the audit procedures and findings related to other inventories that are intended for sale. This form helps ensure that all non-standard inventory items are properly valued and accounted for in the financial statements.\n\n**When to Complete**:  \nThis form should be completed during the onboarding phase of an audit, typically after initial inventory identification and before detailed testing. It is used when there are inventories other than standard stock items (like raw materials or finished goods) that need special attention.\n\n**Key Fields**:  \n1. **Description of Inventory**: Clearly describe what the inventory consists of.\n2. **Location**: Specify where the inventory is stored.\n3. **Quantity and Unit Cost**: Record the quantity on hand and the unit cost to ensure accurate valuation.\n4. **Market Value**: Determine if there are any market value adjustments needed, especially for items that may not be sold at their original cost.\n5. **Audit Procedures Applied**: Document the specific audit procedures performed to verify the inventory.\n\n**Common Mistakes**:  \n1. **Omitting Descriptions**: Forgetting to provide a detailed description of the inventory can lead to misunderstandings about what is being audited.\n2. **Incorrect Valuation**: Using incorrect valuation methods or failing to apply market adjustments can result in misstated inventory values.\n3. **Lack of Documentation**: Not documenting the audit procedures applied thoroughly can compromise the audit trail and reduce the reliability of the findings.\n\n**Connections**:  \nThis form is typically used early in the audit process and may feed into other forms that require detailed inventory valuation, such as 110.",
