    "expertNote": "# Form 130.110: Accounts receivable confirmation - Supplementary Procedures\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: revenue | **Type**: procedure\n**ISA**: ISA 505, ISA 530 | **Connections**: 1 in, 6 out\n\n---\n\n### CaseWare Audit Form 130.110: Accounts Receivable Confirmation - Supplementary Procedures\n\n#### Purpose:\nThis form is used to document supplementary procedures related to accounts receivable confirmations. It helps ensure that the auditor has performed adequate testing and verification of the accuracy and completeness of accounts receivable.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nComplete this form during the onboarding phase, specifically when you need to perform additional or alternative audit procedures for confirming accounts receivable. This might be necessary if standard confirmation procedures are not feasible or reliable.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Type of Supplementary Procedure**: Clearly specify what supplementary procedure is being performed (e.g., inspection of supporting documents, inquiries of management).\n2. **Description of Procedure**: Provide a detailed description of how the procedure was conducted.\n3. **Evidence Obtained**: Document the evidence gathered during the procedure and its relevance to confirming accounts receivable.\n4. **Conclusion**: Summarize the findings from the supplementary procedures and their impact on the audit opinion.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Omitting Details**: Trainees often skip over detailed descriptions of the procedures, which can lead to incomplete documentation.\n2. **Ignoring Evidence**: Not documenting all relevant evidence or failing to explain how it supports the conclusion.\n3. **Lack of Clarity**: Providing vague conclusions that do not clearly reflect the findings and their implications.\n\n#### Connections:\nThis form is typically referenced after initial confirmation efforts (Form ID 1) and before finalizing the audit opinion (Form ID 6). It ensures that all necessary steps are documented to support the accuracy of acc",
