    "expertNote": "# Form 130: Receivables, current leadsheet\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: revenue | **Type**: leadsheet\n**ISA**: N/A | **Connections**: 0 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### CaseWare Audit Form ID: 130 - Receivables, Current Leadsheet\n\n#### Purpose:\nThe Receivables, Current Leadsheet is a foundational document used to summarize key details about the client's receivables at the start of an audit. It helps in understanding the current state of accounts receivable and serves as a starting point for further detailed analysis.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nThis form should be completed during the onboarding phase of the audit, typically before diving into detailed substantive testing. It is useful when you first gather information about the client’s receivables or after any significant changes in the receivables portfolio.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Total Receivables**: This field captures the total amount of receivables at a specific date and is crucial for understanding the scale of the receivables.\n2. **Aging Analysis**: Breakdown by age categories (e.g., current, 30-60 days overdue, etc.) helps identify potential bad debts and credit risk.\n3. **Major Customers**: Listing significant customers can highlight concentration risks and help focus audit efforts where needed.\n4. **Credit Policy Summary**: A brief description of the client’s credit policies aids in assessing compliance with internal controls.\n5. **Receivables Turnover Ratio**: This ratio helps evaluate how efficiently the company is collecting its receivables.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Ignoring Aging Analysis**: Not filling out the aging analysis can lead to overlooking significant overdue accounts and potential write-offs.\n2. **Failing to Update Total Receivables**: Using outdated figures for total receivables can result in inaccurate risk assessments.\n3. **Overlooking Credit Policy Details**: Missing credit policy information can impair your ability to assess internal controls effectively.\n\n#### Connections:\nThis form is",
