    "expertNote": "# Form 140.115: Cash count procedures\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: cash | **Type**: procedure\n**ISA**: ISA 330, ISA 500 | **Connections**: 0 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### CaseWare Audit Form 140.115: Cash Count Procedures\n\n#### Purpose:\nForm ID 140.115 is used to document the procedures an auditor follows during a cash count. This form ensures that all necessary steps are taken to verify the accuracy of cash on hand, which is crucial for assessing the financial statements' reliability.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nThis form should be completed during the initial stages of the audit, typically after planning and before substantive testing. It is used when conducting physical counts of cash in the client's possession, such as at bank branches or headquarters.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Date and Time of Cash Count**: Record the exact date and time to ensure accuracy and traceability.\n2. **Location of Cash Count**: Specify where the cash count took place to provide context for the audit.\n3. **Personnel Present**: Note who was present during the count, including auditors and client personnel, to verify the integrity of the process.\n4. **Amounts Counted**: Document the total amount counted in various denominations to ensure completeness and accuracy.\n5. **Observations and Comments**: Include any observations or comments that might affect the audit findings.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Skipping Detailed Documentation**: Trainees often skip documenting specific details, such as the exact time of the count or personnel present, which can lead to incomplete records.\n2. **Inconsistent Counting Procedures**: Not following a consistent method for counting cash can result in discrepancies and errors.\n3. **Failing to Reconcile Counts**: Forgetting to reconcile the physical count with recorded balances can miss significant issues.\n\n#### Connections:\nThis form is foundational for other audit procedures, such as verifying bank statements (Form 140.120) or assessing internal controls over ca",
