    "expertNote": "# Form 140.120: Cash and cash equivalents - Substantive analytical procedures\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: cash | **Type**: procedure\n**ISA**: ISA 330, ISA 500 | **Connections**: 0 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of CaseWare Audit Form 140.120: Cash and cash equivalents - Substantive analytical procedures\n\n#### Purpose:\nForm 140.120 is used to perform substantive analytical procedures on cash and cash equivalents during the audit process. This helps auditors identify unusual patterns or unexpected changes that could indicate misstatements.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nThis form should be completed during the planning phase of the audit, typically after understanding the client's business and internal controls. It’s particularly useful when there are significant fluctuations in cash balances or discrepancies between expected and actual figures.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Cash Balances**: Enter the opening and closing balances for cash and cash equivalents.\n2. **Reconciling Items**: List any reconciling items that affect cash balances, such as deposits in transit or bank charges.\n3. **Analytical Procedures**: Describe the analytical procedures performed, including ratios and comparisons to prior periods or industry standards.\n4. **Results and Evaluation**: Document the results of your analysis and evaluate whether they are reasonable or require further investigation.\n5. **Conclusion**: Summarize your conclusions regarding the reasonableness of cash balances based on your analysis.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Ignoring Reconciling Items**: Forgetting to include reconciling items can lead to inaccurate cash balance figures.\n2. **Lack of Analysis**: Simply stating results without proper analysis and evaluation can miss red flags.\n3. **Overlooking Industry Standards**: Not comparing results with industry standards or prior periods may miss significant changes.\n\n#### Connections:\nThis form is often completed before detailed substantive tests on cash transactions (e.g., F",
