    "expertNote": "# Form 200.120: Equity - Substantive analytical procedures\n\n**Phase**: 4_testing | **Category**: equity | **Type**: procedure\n**ISA**: ISA 330, ISA 500 | **Connections**: 0 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of CaseWare Audit Form 200.120: Equity - Substantive Analytical Procedures\n\n#### Purpose:\nForm 200.120 is used to perform substantive analytical procedures on equity accounts during the testing phase of an audit. This helps auditors assess the reasonableness and consistency of financial data, ensuring that there are no material misstatements.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nThis form should be completed after initial tests of controls and when substantive analytical procedures are required to gather evidence about the amounts recorded in equity accounts. It is typically used during the detailed testing phase of an audit.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Description of Analytical Procedure**: Clearly describe what you did, such as comparing current year figures with prior years or industry benchmarks.\n2. **Expected Results**: Outline what you expected to find based on historical data or industry standards.\n3. **Actual Results**: Record the actual results obtained from your analysis.\n4. **Differences and Explanations**: Document any significant differences between expected and actual results, along with explanations for these discrepancies.\n5. **Conclusion**: Summarize whether the analytical procedures support the reasonableness of the equity account balances.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Ignoring Significant Differences**: Trainees often overlook or inadequately explain significant differences between expected and actual results.\n2. **Lack of Detail in Descriptions**: Not providing enough detail about the analytical procedure performed can lead to misunderstandings.\n3. **Failing to Document Conclusions**: Forgetting to draw conclusions based on the analysis can result in incomplete audit documentation.\n\n#### Connections:\nThis form is typically completed after reviewing internal contro",
