    "expertNote": "# Form 200.4: Equity - Retained earnings\n\n**Phase**: 4_testing | **Category**: equity | **Type**: worksheet\n**ISA**: N/A | **Connections**: 0 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of CaseWare Audit Form 200.4: Equity - Retained Earnings\n\n**Purpose**:  \nForm 200.4 is used to document the audit procedures related to retained earnings, ensuring that the financial statements accurately reflect the company's equity position.\n\n**When to Complete**:  \nThis form should be completed during the testing phase of the audit, specifically when verifying the accuracy and completeness of retained earnings calculations and disclosures in the financial statements.\n\n**Key Fields**:\n1. **Beginning Balance**: Verify the opening balance of retained earnings from prior period financial statements.\n2. **Additions (Net Income)**: Confirm that net income for the current period has been correctly added to retained earnings.\n3. **Deductions (Dividends Paid)**: Ensure that dividends declared and paid during the period have been accurately deducted from retained earnings.\n4. **Ending Balance**: Calculate and verify the ending balance of retained earnings by reconciling additions and deductions to the beginning balance.\n5. **Adjustments**: Document any necessary adjustments identified during the audit process.\n\n**Common Mistakes**:\n1. **Incorrect Reconciliation**: Failing to properly reconcile the beginning balance, additions, deductions, and adjustments can lead to an incorrect ending balance of retained earnings.\n2. **Omission of Dividends**: Forgetting to deduct dividends paid from retained earnings is a common oversight that affects the accuracy of the equity section.\n3. **Ignoring Prior Period Adjustments**: Not considering prior period corrections or adjustments can result in an inaccurate opening balance.\n\n**Connections**:\nThis form relies on information from other forms, such as those related to net income (Form 200.1) and dividends (Form 200.5). It also feeds into the overall equity section of",
