    "expertNote": "# Form 335.10: Uncorrected misstatements - all\n\n**Phase**: 3_risk | **Category**: general | **Type**: worksheet\n**ISA**: N/A | **Connections**: 1 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of CaseWare Audit Form 335.10: Uncorrected Misstatements - All\n\n#### Purpose:\nForm 335.10 is used to document all uncorrected misstatements identified during the audit process. This form helps ensure that the auditor has considered the impact of these misstatements on the financial statements and the overall audit opinion.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nThis form should be completed after identifying uncorrected misstatements in the financial statements, typically towards the end of the risk assessment phase or as part of the final review process. It is crucial when there are material misstatements that have not been corrected by management.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Misstatement Description**: Clearly describe what the misstatement is and where it appears in the financial statements.\n2. **Amount**: Specify the monetary amount of each uncorrected misstatement.\n3. **Impact on Financial Statements**: Explain how the misstatement affects the financial statements and whether it is material.\n4. **Audit Adjustments Required**: Indicate if any audit adjustments are needed despite the uncorrected misstatements.\n5. **Management’s Response**: Document management's response to the identified misstatements.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Inadequate Description**: Trainees often provide vague descriptions of misstatements, making it difficult for reviewers to understand their impact.\n2. **Ignoring Non-Monetary Misstatements**: Sometimes, non-monetary misstatements are overlooked, which can be equally important in assessing the overall financial statement accuracy.\n3. **Failing to Document Management’s Response**: Not recording management's reaction or comments on identified misstatements can lead to incomplete audit documentation.\n\n#### Connections:\nThis form is typically used after identifying uncorrected misstateme",
