    "expertNote": "# Form 335.11: Unrecorded misstatements - factual\n\n**Phase**: 3_risk | **Category**: general | **Type**: worksheet\n**ISA**: N/A | **Connections**: 1 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### CaseWare Audit Form 335.11: Unrecorded Misstatements - Factual\n\n#### Purpose:\nForm 335.11 is used to identify and document factual misstatements that have been discovered during the audit process. This form helps in assessing the materiality of these misstatements and determining their impact on financial statements.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nThis form should be completed during the risk assessment phase when auditors identify unrecorded transactions or errors that could affect the accuracy of the financial statements. It is crucial to document these findings early to ensure they are considered in the overall audit strategy.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Misstatement Description**: Clearly describe what the misstatement is and how it was discovered.\n2. **Amount**: Specify the monetary amount of the misstatement, if applicable.\n3. **Materiality Assessment**: Evaluate whether the misstatement is material to the financial statements.\n4. **Corrective Actions**: Outline any actions taken or planned to correct the misstatement.\n5. **Audit Evidence**: Document the evidence supporting the identification and assessment of the misstatement.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Incomplete Descriptions**: Trainees often provide vague descriptions that do not clearly explain the nature of the misstatement.\n2. **Ignoring Materiality**: Forgetting to assess whether the misstatement is material can lead to overlooking significant issues.\n3. **Lack of Evidence Documentation**: Not documenting sufficient evidence to support the identification and assessment of the misstatement can weaken the audit trail.\n\n#### Connections:\nThis form feeds into other risk assessment forms, such as 335.01 (Risk Assessment - Overall) and 335.02 (Risk Assessment - Assertions), by providing specific examples of factual misstatements that influence the overa",
