    "expertNote": "# Form 335.13: Uncorrected misstatements - judgmental\n\n**Phase**: 3_risk | **Category**: general | **Type**: worksheet\n**ISA**: ISA 330, ISA 500 | **Connections**: 1 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of CaseWare Audit Form 335.13: Uncorrected Misstatements - Judgmental\n\n#### Purpose:\nForm 335.13 is used to document uncorrected misstatements that are significant but not material individually or collectively. This form helps auditors assess the impact of these misstatements on the financial statements and audit opinion.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nComplete this form during the risk assessment phase, typically after identifying and evaluating misstatements in the financial statements. It is used when you have identified uncorrected misstatements that need to be evaluated for materiality and their effect on the overall financial statement presentation.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Description of Misstatement**: Clearly describe what the misstatement is.\n2. **Amount**: Specify the amount of the misstatement.\n3. **Materiality Assessment**: Evaluate whether the misstatement, individually or collectively, affects the materiality of the financial statements.\n4. **Impact on Financial Statements**: Describe how the uncorrected misstatement impacts the financial statements and disclosures.\n5. **Audit Adjustments Needed**: Indicate if any adjustments are needed despite the misstatements being uncorrected.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Inadequate Description**: Trainees often provide vague descriptions of misstatements, making it difficult to understand their impact.\n2. **Ignoring Materiality**: Failing to properly assess whether the misstatement is material can lead to incorrect conclusions about the financial statements.\n3. **Overlooking Adjustments**: Not considering necessary adjustments even if the misstatements are uncorrected can result in an inaccurate audit opinion.\n\n#### Connections:\nThis form feeds into the overall risk assessment and judgmental process, influencing the auditor's de",
