    "expertNote": "# Form 400-4: Optimiser checklist - Profiles 2, 3 and 4\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: general | **Type**: checklist\n**ISA**: ISA 220 (Revised), ISQM 2 | **Connections**: 0 in, 3 out\n\n---\n\n### CaseWare Audit Form 400-4: Optimiser Checklist - Profiles 2, 3 and 4\n\n#### Purpose:\nThis checklist helps auditors ensure that the client’s financial reporting framework is correctly identified and that appropriate audit procedures are planned for clients classified under Profiles 2, 3, or 4. It serves as a guide during the onboarding phase to set up the audit effectively.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nComplete this form when you start an engagement with a new client or at the beginning of each audit cycle if there have been significant changes in the client's operations or financial reporting framework. This ensures that all necessary information is gathered and considered before planning further audit procedures.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Client Profile Selection**: Choose the correct profile (2, 3, or 4) based on the client’s size, complexity, and risk factors.\n2. **Financial Reporting Framework**: Identify whether the client follows IFRS, US GAAP, or another framework.\n3. **Materiality Levels**: Set appropriate materiality levels for the audit to ensure that significant financial misstatements are detected.\n4. **Risk Assessment**: Evaluate key risks related to the client’s operations and financial reporting processes.\n5. **Audit Procedures**: List planned audit procedures tailored to the identified profile and risk assessment.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Incorrect Profile Selection**: Choosing an incorrect profile can lead to inadequate or excessive audit procedures, affecting the quality of the audit.\n2. **Overlooking Materiality Levels**: Failing to set accurate materiality levels can result in overlooking significant misstatements.\n3. **Neglecting Risk Assessment**: Skipping a thorough risk assessment can miss critical areas that need more detailed examination.\n\n#### Co",
