    "expertNote": "# Form 400-6: Optimiser checklist - Profile 1\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: general | **Type**: checklist\n**ISA**: N/A | **Connections**: 0 in, 1 out\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of CaseWare Audit Form 400-6: Optimiser Checklist - Profile 1\n\n#### Purpose:\nForm 400-6 is used to ensure that the audit team has a clear understanding of the client's business profile and risk assessment before diving into detailed procedures. It helps set the stage for an efficient and effective audit process.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nThis form should be completed during the onboarding phase, typically at the very beginning of the engagement, once you have received all necessary information about the client but before starting any substantive audit work.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Client Profile**: This section requires a detailed description of the client's business activities, industry, and size. It’s crucial for understanding the context in which the company operates.\n2. **Risk Assessment**: Identify key risks associated with the client’s operations and financial reporting. Accurately identifying these helps tailor your audit approach.\n3. **Materiality Levels**: Set materiality thresholds to determine what issues are significant enough to require further investigation.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Overlooking Key Risks**: Trainees often skip over or understate risks, leading to an insufficient risk assessment and potentially missing critical issues.\n2. **Inadequate Client Profile Information**: Not providing a comprehensive description of the client’s business can result in misunderstandings about the audit scope and objectives.\n\n#### Connections:\nThis form feeds into other audit forms by setting the groundwork for more detailed procedures. For example, the materiality levels set here will influence the design of substantive tests (e.g., sample sizes) on subsequent forms.\n\n#### ISA Context:\nWhile no specific ISA references are listed, this form aligns with several ISAs that emphasize und",
