    "expertNote": "# Form 400.2: Non-interest bearing borrowings\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: general | **Type**: worksheet\n**ISA**: ISA 330, ISA 500 | **Connections**: 0 in, 2 out\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of CaseWare Audit Form 400.2: Non-interest bearing borrowings\n\n**Purpose**: This worksheet helps auditors identify, assess, and document non-interest bearing borrowings (NIBs) during the onboarding phase of an audit. It ensures that all NIBs are properly recorded and disclosed in the financial statements.\n\n**When to Complete**: You should complete this form early in the audit process, typically during the planning or initial assessment stage, especially when you suspect or have evidence of non-interest bearing borrowings in the client's transactions.\n\n**Key Fields**:\n1. **Description of Borrowing**: Clearly describe each NIB to ensure accuracy.\n2. **Terms and Conditions**: Detail any conditions or terms associated with the borrowing that could affect its classification or disclosure.\n3. **Amounts Involved**: Record the exact amounts involved in each NIB transaction.\n4. **Related Party Information**: Identify if the borrower is a related party, as this can impact how the borrowing should be disclosed.\n5. **Impact on Financial Statements**: Assess and document any potential impacts of these borrowings on the financial statements.\n\n**Common Mistakes**:\n1. **Omitting Details**: Trainees often skip over detailed descriptions or terms and conditions, leading to incomplete information.\n2. **Ignoring Related Party Transactions**: Not recognizing related party transactions can result in improper disclosure.\n3. **Failing to Update Amounts**: Forging ahead without verifying the accuracy of amounts involved can lead to incorrect financial reporting.\n\n**Connections**: This form is foundational for other audit forms that deal with borrowings and disclosures, such as those covering debt agreements (Form ID 400.1) or related party transactions (Form ID 500.2). It provides critical informat",
