    "expertNote": "# Form 425.120: Payables - Substantive analytical procedures\n\n**Phase**: 4_testing | **Category**: expenditure | **Type**: procedure\n**ISA**: ISA 330, ISA 500 | **Connections**: 0 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of CaseWare Audit Form 425.120: Payables - Substantive Analytical Procedures\n\n#### Purpose:\nForm 425.120 is used to perform substantive analytical procedures on payables during the testing phase of an audit. This helps in identifying unusual patterns or discrepancies that could indicate misstatements.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nThis form should be completed after initial data gathering and preliminary reviews, typically during the detailed testing phase when you are analyzing payables for accuracy and completeness.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Description of Analytical Procedure**: Clearly describe what analytical procedure is being performed.\n2. **Expected Results**: Outline what results you expect based on historical data or industry standards.\n3. **Actual Results**: Record the actual results obtained from your analysis.\n4. **Tolerances and Thresholds**: Define acceptable variances between expected and actual results.\n5. **Evaluation of Differences**: Document any differences found and how they were evaluated.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Ignoring Small Variances**: Trainees sometimes overlook small discrepancies, which can be significant.\n2. **Lack of Documentation**: Failing to document the rationale behind analytical procedures or the evaluation of results.\n3. **Inconsistent Expectations**: Not setting realistic expectations based on reliable data sources.\n\n#### Connections:\nThis form is typically used after reviewing detailed records and before concluding on payables. It feeds into the overall audit opinion by providing evidence that supports the accuracy and completeness assertions for payables.\n\n#### ISA Context:\nForm 425.120 satisfies requirements under ISA 330 (Auditor’s Responses to Assessed Risks) and ISA 500 (Audit Evidence). It ensures that substantive a",
