    "expertNote": "# Form 5009: Consolidation procedures\n\n**Phase**: 2_planning | **Category**: group_audit | **Type**: checklist\n**ISA**: ISA 600 | **Connections**: 0 in, 1 out\n\n---\n\n### CaseWare Audit Form ID: 5009 - Consolidation Procedures\n\n**Purpose**:  \nForm 5009 helps auditors plan the procedures needed to audit a group's financial statements by focusing on consolidation processes. It ensures that all necessary steps are considered, minimizing risks and improving audit quality.\n\n**When to Complete**:  \nThis form is completed during the planning phase of an audit when dealing with group audits. Specifically, it should be filled out after initial risk assessment and before detailed audit procedures begin.\n\n**Key Fields**:\n1. **Identify Consolidation Method**: Clearly specify whether the entity uses the equity method or the proportionate consolidation method.\n2. **Materiality Thresholds**: Define materiality levels for different components of the group to ensure that all significant items are addressed.\n3. **Significant Transactions**: List any significant intra-group transactions and their impact on financial statements.\n4. **Risk Assessment**: Assess risks related to consolidation, such as errors in allocation or misstatements.\n5. **Procedures to Be Performed**: Outline specific procedures for auditing the consolidation process.\n\n**Common Mistakes**:\n1. **Ignoring Intra-Group Transactions**: Trainees often overlook significant intra-group transactions, which can lead to material misstatements.\n2. **Failing to Update Materiality Levels**: Not adjusting materiality thresholds based on changes in financial performance or group structure can result in incomplete audits.\n3. **Overlooking Risk Assessment**: Skipping detailed risk assessment for consolidation processes can miss critical areas that need more scrutiny.\n\n**Connections**:\nThis form is crucial as it feeds into the overall audit strategy and plan (Form ID 5001) by providing specific procedures related to consolidation. It al",
