    "expertNote": "# Form AOCS: Audit Optimiser confirmation report\n\n**Phase**: 5_completion | **Category**: it_controls | **Type**: report\n**ISA**: N/A | **Connections**: 0 in, 2 out\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of the AOCS Form: Audit Optimiser Confirmation Report\n\n**Purpose**: The AOCS form is used to document the confirmation process for IT controls during the completion phase of an audit. It ensures that all necessary confirmations have been received and evaluated.\n\n**When to Complete**: This form should be completed at the end of the audit, after all IT control testing has been performed and confirmations from relevant parties (like system administrators or IT staff) have been obtained. It is used to summarize the status of these confirmations.\n\n**Key Fields**:\n1. **Control Description**: Clearly describe the IT control being confirmed.\n2. **Confirmation Status**: Indicate whether the confirmation was received, pending, or not required.\n3. **Comments/Findings**: Provide any relevant comments or findings related to the confirmation process.\n4. **Date of Confirmation**: Record the date when the confirmation was received.\n5. **Responsible Person**: Identify who is responsible for obtaining and evaluating the confirmation.\n\n**Common Mistakes**:\n1. **Inaccurate Control Descriptions**: Trainees often provide vague or overly technical descriptions that do not clearly convey what the control is.\n2. **Omitting Comments**: Forgetting to include important comments about discrepancies or issues found during the confirmation process can lead to misunderstandings.\n3. **Incorrect Dates**: Entering incorrect dates for when confirmations were received can disrupt the audit timeline and affect the overall assessment.\n\n**Connections**: The AOCS form is typically referenced by other forms that require documentation of IT control confirmations, such as the IT General Controls Report (ITGC) or the Final Audit Summary (FAS). It ensures that all necessary confirmations are accounted for before finalizing the ",
