    "expertNote": "# Form CTRLM.: Control matrix\n\n**Phase**: 3_risk | **Category**: general | **Type**: worksheet\n**ISA**: N/A | **Connections**: 0 in, 3 out\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of CaseWare Audit Form CTRLM: Control Matrix\n\n#### Purpose:\nThe Control Matrix (CTRLM) is a worksheet used to document the controls identified during the risk assessment phase of an audit. It helps auditors understand and evaluate the effectiveness of internal controls that mitigate risks.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nYou should complete this form after identifying and documenting relevant controls in the risk assessment stage, typically during planning or early execution phases of the audit.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Control Description**: Clearly describe each control.\n2. **Risk Addressed**: Specify which specific risks the control is designed to mitigate.\n3. **Evaluation of Control Design**: Assess whether the control design is appropriate and effective.\n4. **Testing Required**: Indicate if testing is needed to determine the operating effectiveness of the control.\n5. **Residual Risk**: Evaluate the risk remaining after considering the identified controls.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Incomplete Descriptions**: Failing to provide a detailed description can lead to misunderstandings about what the control entails.\n2. **Overlooking Residual Risk**: Not assessing residual risk accurately can result in underestimating audit risks.\n3. **Ignoring Testing Requirements**: Forgetting to specify whether testing is needed can leave gaps in the audit process.\n\n#### Connections:\nThe Control Matrix feeds into other forms such as the Audit Program (AUDPROG) and the Test of Controls (TOC). It provides a foundation for designing test procedures and evaluating control effectiveness, which are critical for the overall audit plan.\n\n#### ISA Context:\nWhile no specific ISA references are listed, this form aligns with several ISAs, particularly ISA 315 (Understanding the Entity and Its Environment and Assessing Risks) and ISA 330 (The Au",
