    "expertNote": "# Form EXP: Expenditure Cycle\n\n**Phase**: 4_testing | **Category**: expenditure | **Type**: leadsheet\n**ISA**: N/A | **Connections**: 0 in, 0 out\n\n---\n\n### CaseWare Audit Form: Expenditure Cycle (EXP)\n\n#### Purpose:\nThe EXP form is used to document the testing procedures for the expenditure cycle, ensuring that expenses are properly recorded, authorized, and supported by appropriate documentation.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nThis form should be completed during the testing phase of an audit when you're verifying the accuracy and completeness of expenditures. It's typically filled out after designing your test procedures and before performing substantive tests on transactions.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Description of Transaction**: Clearly describe the type of expenditure being tested.\n2. **Test Procedures**: Detail the specific steps taken to verify the transaction, such as reviewing supporting documents or tracing entries.\n3. **Sample Size**: Indicate how many transactions were selected for testing.\n4. **Findings and Conclusions**: Record any discrepancies found and your conclusions regarding the accuracy of the expenditures.\n5. **Audit Evidence Attached**: Note if relevant audit evidence is attached or referenced.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Omitting Test Procedures**: Forgetting to detail the specific test procedures can lead to incomplete documentation.\n2. **Ignoring Findings**: Not recording discrepancies or issues found during testing can result in missed audit adjustments.\n3. **Failing to Document Sample Size**: Not specifying how many transactions were tested can undermine the reliability of your audit conclusions.\n\n#### Connections:\nThe EXP form is connected to other forms like the General Ledger (GL) and Substantive Testing (ST) forms. It provides detailed testing information that supports findings on these broader forms, ensuring a comprehensive audit trail.\n\n#### ISA Context:\nWhile no specific ISA references are listed, this form aligns with ISAs related to sub",
