    "expertNote": "# Form 590: Preliminary Engagement Scoping - Single Entity\n\n**Phase**: 1_onboarding | **Category**: general | **Type**: worksheet\n**ISA**: ISA 315, ISA 320 | **Connections**: 0 in, 3 out\n\n---\n\n### CaseWare Audit Form ID: 590 - Preliminary Engagement Scoping - Single Entity\n\n#### Purpose:\nForm ID 590 is used to outline the scope of the audit for a single entity at the very beginning of an engagement. It helps set expectations and guides the planning process.\n\n#### When to Complete:\nThis form should be completed during the initial onboarding phase, typically before any substantive fieldwork begins. It’s essential when you’re first accepting or re-engaging with a client.\n\n#### Key Fields:\n1. **Client Information**: Ensure you have the correct name, address, and contact details.\n2. **Engagement Objectives**: Clearly define what you aim to achieve with this audit.\n3. **Scope of Work**: Specify which financial statements will be audited and any other services provided.\n4. **Materiality Levels**: Set initial materiality levels for the engagement.\n5. **Risk Assessment**: Identify potential risks that could impact the audit.\n\n#### Common Mistakes:\n1. **Incomplete Client Information**: Forgetting to fill in all necessary details can lead to confusion later.\n2. **Unclear Engagement Objectives**: Vague objectives can result in unclear scope and direction.\n3. **Overlooking Materiality Levels**: Not setting these early on can cause issues during the audit.\n\n#### Connections:\nThis form feeds into other planning forms like Form ID 600 (Risk Assessment) and Form ID 700 (Audit Program). It sets the foundation for all subsequent work, so it’s crucial to get right from the start.\n\n#### ISA Context:\nForm ID 590 satisfies requirements under ISA 315 (Identifying and Assessing the Risks of Material Misstatement) by helping identify risks early. It also aligns with ISA 320 (Materiality in Planning and Performing an Audit) by establishing materiality levels at the outset.\n\nBy carefully compl",
